Returning `''` (empty string) for the no-filters case creates a mismatch between the function's declared return type (`string`) and the `null`-signalling needed by its call site. The call site compensates with `tagsValue ?? null`, which is the wrong operator for this (see the related bug on line 409 of stories.ts).

A cleaner contract would be to return `null` instead of `''` for the empty case and update the return type to `string | null`:
```ts
export const serializeTagsParam = (included: Tag[], excluded: Tag[]): string | null => {
  if (!included.length && !excluded.length) {
    return null;
  }
  // ...
};
```
This makes the call site `tagsValue ?? null` both correct and self-documenting.

The migration loop calls `get(storage)` for both local and session storage, and then `get(store.local)` / `get(store.session)` are called again a few lines later for the actual merge. Each of these reads deserialises from `localStorage`/`sessionStorage`. Caching the result of the first read avoids the double I/O:
```ts
const local = get(store.local);
const session = get(store.session);
for (const [storage, persisted] of [[store.local, local], [store.session, session]] as const) {
  if ('includedTagFilters' in persisted || 'excludedTagFilters' in persisted) {
    const { includedTagFilters: _i, excludedTagFilters: _e, ...rest } = persisted;
    set(storage, rest);
  }
}
return { ...base, ...local, ...session };
```
(The existing test comment that explains "each storage is read twice" would then also need updating.)
